# Getting started

Pau includes a browser-based component workbench for developing and reviewing
shared interface components without starting the desktop app or a local agent.

## Check the workspace

Install dependencies, then run the JavaScript and Rust checks:

```bash
pnpm install
pnpm check
cargo check --workspace
```

Generate the TypeScript wire types after a Rust protocol type changes:

```bash
pnpm generate:protocol
```

## Install Pi

Pau requires Pi 0.87.1 or newer. Install Pi globally if `pi --version` does not
report a supported release:

```bash
npm install --global @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent
pi --version
```

Pau finds `pi` on `PATH` unless you configure another executable. It reads user
configuration from `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` or `~/.pi/agent`, project
configuration from `<workspace>/.pi`, and session storage from
`PI_CODING_AGENT_SESSION_DIR` or the user configuration directory. The version
check does not start a Pi session.

## Review interface components

Start Storybook with hot reload:

```bash
pnpm dev:ui
```

Use its toolbar to change the theme, viewport, motion preference, and available
agent capabilities. Run the interaction, accessibility, and browser component
tests before submitting interface changes:

```bash
pnpm test:ui
```

Build the static workbench with `pnpm build:ui`.

Open **Sessions / Session view** to review empty, starting, running, waiting,
failed, and stopped sessions. In a running session, press `Ctrl+Enter` or
`Command+Enter` to send a prompt and `Escape` to interrupt. Focus a session in
the workspace rail and use the up or down arrow key to switch sessions.

## Run the local app

The desktop app generates a new local API token each time it starts. Its webview
uses that token for HTTP requests and WebSocket connections. Requests without
the token cannot read the health or version endpoints or open the event stream.

```bash
pnpm --filter @stormlightlabs/pau-desktop dev
```

For browser development, generate a token with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Set the
same value as `PAU_API_TOKEN` for the Rust server and Vite development server.
Vite adds it to proxied API requests; do not put the token in source files or
commit it.

```bash
PAU_API_TOKEN="$TOKEN" cargo run -p pau-server
PAU_API_TOKEN="$TOKEN" pnpm --filter @stormlightlabs/pau-web dev
```
